{"page_content": "\u201cThe only significant disappointment with the project was with the diagnostics, \nparticularly with respect to gas temperature and soot volume fraction. They were not at \nthe level that the team would have hoped.  Much of this was due to the fact that the \ndiagnostics were outdated by the time they were used in our experiments on the ISS, and \nthus the diagnostics and calibrations were not at the level that we expect in a laboratory \nexperiment.  Of course, the ISS has constraints that limit the ability to incorporate state-\nof-the-art diagnostics, and this likely made this weakness unavoidable, but it would have \nbeen valuable to have had more flexibility in terms of being able to upgrade the \ndiagnostics with more modern cameras and instrumentation.\u201d \n \nACME\u2019s sintered spherical burners did not always yield spherically symmetric flames which are \ncentered on the burner\u2019s center, where that combination is important for true spherical \nsymmetry.  While nominally spherical at moderate flow rates, the flame\u2019s center became \nincreasing offset from the burner center as the flow rate was increased.  It is very likely that the \nsintered attachment of the sphere to the supply tube is more porous than the sintered sphere", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 18}}